Description: hypervisor: riscv64: drop Irqchip and IrqRouting capability checks
 RISC-V KVM does not expose KVM_CAP_IRQCHIP or KVM_CAP_IRQ_ROUTING.
 Interrupt handling on RISC-V uses the AIA (Advanced Interrupt
 Architecture) through KVM_DEV_TYPE_RISCV_AIA, not the in-kernel
 irqchip interface used on x86 and ARM.
 .
 The riscv64 check_required_kvm_extensions list was copied verbatim
 from the aarch64 version when RISC-V support was introduced and was
 never adjusted, causing an unconditional panic at VM creation on any
 RISC-V host.
 .
 Remove Cap::Irqchip and Cap::IrqRouting from the riscv64 capability
 check list so that only capabilities actually reported by the kernel
 are required.
Author: Gauthier Jolly <contact@gjolly.fr>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-07-06
---
--- a/hypervisor/src/kvm/riscv64/mod.rs
+++ b/hypervisor/src/kvm/riscv64/mod.rs
@@ -110,9 +110,7 @@
     // SetGuestDebug is required but some kernels have it implemented without the capability flag.
     check_extension!(Cap::ImmediateExit);
     check_extension!(Cap::Ioeventfd);
-    check_extension!(Cap::Irqchip);
     check_extension!(Cap::Irqfd);
-    check_extension!(Cap::IrqRouting);
     check_extension!(Cap::MpState);
     check_extension!(Cap::OneReg);
     check_extension!(Cap::UserMemory);
